ls, ratings, or spending signals point toward Democrats. State Republican cohesion in 2026 reinforces the fundamentals rather than cutting against them.", "watch": "The first credible statewide poll, or a large national swing toward Democrats, would be the most likely trigger for revision.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "North Dakota remains one of the most Republican states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly eighteen points toward the GOP. No incumbent is on the ballot in 2028 and neither party has a defined nominee yet, so this is a generic contest between the parties. No public polls of the state, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices are available, and neither side has reported fundraising or outside spending.\n\nIn the absence of state-specific evidence, the state's Republican fundamentals dominate the picture. Local party politics show no signs of fracture: North Dakota Republicans favored J.D. Vance in a 2028 straw poll and publicly pushed Governor Doug Burgum away from a presidential run. A Democratic win would require a national shift far larger than anything in the recent evidence, and the Republican nominee is the heavy favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0273, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0558, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0549, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0572, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0505, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0515, "margin": -18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0556, "margin": -18.0}]}